## What to do if HTTPS cloning doesn't work

## The GnuTLS recv error (-54) combined with an early EOF indicates that your local network or proxy is dropping the HTTPS connection mid-transfer due to the volume of data being downloaded. [1, 2, 3] 
You can resolve this Git cloning error using one of the step-by-step methods outlined below.
## Method 1: Shallow Clone (Recommended Workflow)
A shallow clone skips the repository's entire history and downloads only the latest snapshot. This drastically decreases the transfer size, bypassing network drops. [4] 
Run these commands in your terminal:

# 1. Clone only the latest commit
git clone --depth 1 https://github.com
# 2. Move into the directory
cd graphify
# 3. Pull the remaining history incrementally (optional)
git fetch --unshallow

## Method 2: Expand the Git Network Buffer
If Git handles large payloads with default network configurations, the underlying curl buffer can overflow. You can solve this by increasing the memory limit to 500MB and turning off compression. [4, 5, 6] 
Execute the following configuration parameters before retrying your clone:

# Increase the post buffer size to 500MB
git config --global http.postBuffer 524288000
# Disable compression temporarily 
git config --global core.compression 0
# Retry your clone command
git clone https://github.com/Graphify-Labs/graphify

## Method 3: Switch to the SSH Protocol
If your internet service provider or firewall restricts massive data chunks over port 443 (HTTPS), cloning via SSH protocol entirely avoids the GnuTLS web layer. [2] 

# Ensure you have your SSH keys linked to your GitHub account first
git clone git@github.com:Graphify-Labs/graphify.git

If you encounter an issue after trying these steps, let me know:

* Are you on a corporate/restricted network or using a VPN?
* Did the --depth 1 command succeed or fail at the exact same stage? [1, 7, 8] 


